    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "practice": null,
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Greek gave English its words for feelings. <b>path</b> means “feeling” (and also “suffering”): <i>sympathy</i> is feeling with someone. <b>phil</b> means “love”: <i>philosophy</i> is the love of wisdom, and the city name Philadelphia means “brotherly love.” Its opposite, <b>miso</b> (or <b>mis-</b>), means “hate.” And <b>anthrop</b> means “human being.”"
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-scene",
        "opts": {
          "scene": {
            "bg": "library",
            "cast": [
              {
                "who": "lila",
                "x": 42,
                "s": 1.3,
                "pose": "read",
                "mood": "love",
                "face": "right"
              }
            ],
            "props": [
              {
                "kind": "books",
                "x": 72,
                "s": 1.1
              },
              {
                "kind": "books",
                "x": 86,
                "s": 0.9
              }
            ],
            "fx": [
              {
                "kind": "hearts",
                "on": "c0",
                "at": "head"
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              {
                "text": "BIBLIO-PHILE: book lover",
                "x": 50,
                "y": 92
              }
            ],
            "alt": "A girl in a library reads a book with hearts floating around her head."
          }
        },
        "cap": "A girl in a library reads a book with hearts floating around her head."
      },
      {
        "t": "key",
        "html": "<b>path</b> = <b>feeling, suffering</b>. <b>phil</b> = <b>love</b>. <b>miso</b>, <b>mis-</b> = <b>hate</b>. <b>anthrop</b> = <b>human being</b>."
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Put a prefix on <i>path</i> and you get a whole family of feelings: <b>apathy</b> (no feeling), <b>empathy</b> (feeling in another’s place), <b>sympathy</b> (feeling with), <b>antipathy</b> (feeling against). Put <i>phil</i> or <i>miso</i> on <i>anthrop</i> and you get two opposite people: the <b>philanthropist</b>, who loves humankind, and the <b>misanthrope</b>, who dislikes people."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "The parts and their words"
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "roots": [
            "path",
            "phil"
          ]
        },
        "cap": "path (feeling) and phil (love)."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "roots": [
            "miso",
            "anthrop"
          ]
        },
        "cap": "miso (hate) and anthrop (human being)."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "Words taken apart"
      },
      {
        "t": "table",
        "head": [
          "Word and meaning",
          "Parts, and what they say"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "<b>apathy</b><br>lack of interest or concern",
            "a- <i>without</i> + <b>path</b> <i>feeling</i> + -y<br>→ “without feeling”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>empathy</b><br>the ability to share and understand another’s feelings",
            "em- <i>in</i> + <b>path</b> <i>feeling</i> + -y<br>→ “feeling in (another’s place)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>antipathy</b><br>a strong dislike",
            "anti- <i>against</i> + <b>path</b> <i>feeling</i> + -y<br>→ “feeling against”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>pathos</b><br>a quality that stirs pity or sadness",
            "<b>path</b> <i>suffering</i> + -os<br>→ “suffering (that stirs pity)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>telepathy</b><br>the supposed sending of thoughts from one mind to another without words",
            "tele <i>far</i> + <b>path</b> <i>feeling</i> + -y<br>→ “feeling from far away”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>philanthropist</b><br>a person who gives generously to help others",
            "<b>phil</b> <i>love</i> + <b>anthrop</b> <i>human being</i> + -ist <i>a person who</i><br>→ “one who loves humankind”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>bibliophile</b><br>a person who loves books",
            "biblio <i>book</i> + <b>phil</b> <i>love</i> + -e<br>→ “a lover of books”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>misanthrope</b><br>a person who dislikes people in general",
            "<b>mis</b> <i>hate</i> + <b>anthrop</b> <i>human being</i> + -e<br>→ “a hater of people”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>anthropology</b><br>the study of human beings and their cultures",
            "<b>anthrop</b> <i>human being</i> + -logy <i>study of</i><br>→ “the study of human beings”"
          ]
        ],
        "cap": "Each word, what it means now, and its parts."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Empathy or sympathy?",
        "q": "Maya’s friend lost a big game. Maya, who lost one last year, says, “I know exactly how that feels.” Is that <b>empathy</b> or <b>sympathy</b>?",
        "steps": [
          "<b>sympathy</b> = <i>sym-</i> (with) + <i>path</i> (feeling): feeling sorry for someone.",
          "<b>empathy</b> = <i>em-</i> (in) + <i>path</i>: feeling from inside the other person’s place, sharing the feeling.",
          "Maya shares the feeling from the inside."
        ],
        "a": "Empathy."
      },
